= Oral allergy syndrome (OAS) - also known as pollen food allergy syndrome

The foods that bother you usually depend on which specific pollen you are allergic to. Here are the most common cross-reactive categories:

=== Birch Tree Pollen
This is the most common cause of OAS. Associated foods include:

Fruits: Apple, pear, peach, nectarine, plum, cherry, fig, apricot, kiwi, lychee, strawberry, persimmon

Nuts: almond, hazelnut, walnut

Vegetables:
- Carrot, celery, parsnip, fennel, green peppers, parsnips, tomato
- beans, peas, peanut, peas, soybean
- basil, chicory, dill, marjoram, oregano, parsley, tarragon, thyme
- anise, caraway, coriander, cumin, paprika

=== Grass Pollen
Associated foods include:

Melons, tomato, orange, peanut, fig, date, pea

=== Ragweed Pollen
Associated foods include:

Banana.

Melons: Cantaloupe, honeydew, watermelon.

Vegetables: Zucchini, cucumber.

== Why does OAS happen?
OAS occurs in people who are allergic to environmental pollens (like trees, grasses, or weeds). These patients have developed antibodies against these pollens; however, the proteins found in certain fresh fruits, vegetables, and tree nuts are structurally very similar to the proteins found in those pollens.

When you eat these specific fresh foods, your immune system gets confused. It mistakes the food proteins for the pollen you are allergic to and triggers usually a localized reaction - commonly itching, tingling, or mild swelling of the lips, mouth, and throat.

== Why Raw Foods Cause Reactions, but Cooked Foods Do Not
You might notice that you can eat a slice of apple pie without any issues, but eating a raw apple makes your mouth itch. The proteins responsible for OAS are very fragile. When the food is heated or processed, the proteins begin to break down and are no longer recognized by your immune system.

Stomach acid also breaks down these fragile proteins quickly, which is why symptoms are usually limited to the mouth and throat and very rarely progress to severe, whole-body reactions like anaphylaxis.

== Other information

- OAS is NOT a reaction to pesticides, insecticides, or the protective wax applied to the skin of fruits and vegetables
- Some varieties of the same fruit may be tolerated; just because you are sensitized to one pollen does not mean you will have OAS to every possible fruit/vegetable associated with it

